- when building websites for other people, how would I hand it off to them without having the burden of server maintenance? Or would that be something I should offer or is there a way to hand it off because if I build 20 websites I wouldn't want to do server maintenance for 20 different websites and monitor their health, etc..
	Default to client-owned hosting: the client owns and pays for the domain, DNS, deployment account, database/storage, email, and payment accounts; you are added as a developer while building. Deliver the source repository to their organization, transfer the deployment, document environment variables and routine updates, then remove your access after a defined handoff window. For ordinary static sites, managed hosting is usually enough; for dynamic sites, use a client-owned managed platform and database rather than a server you personally maintain. Offer ongoing maintenance only as a separate monthly service with a written scope, response times, backups, and update fees. That makes 20 completed client sites 20 client-owned services, not 20 servers you are quietly on call for.
- route 53 for domain... do i need to route traffic to my IP for port forwarding thing?
	Yes. Point Route 53 `A` records for the domain and `www` at the router's public IPv4 address; the router then forwards TCP 80 and 443 to the home server. DNS has no port field, so do not use the server's private `192.168.x.x` address. This needs the static public IP answered in `0_QUESTIONS`, or a dynamic-DNS updater.
- why doesn the scheduler .service need to specify a port or anything
	It is a background worker, not a web server. It starts `run_scheduler.py`, waits for timed jobs, and talks only to the database, email provider, and Stripe API through its environment; no browser or Nginx request connects to it. Only the Gunicorn API service needs port 8001 because Nginx forwards web requests there.
